WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.209 --> 00:00:04.087
This is where the FIFA World Cup
final will soon be held...

00:00:04.421 --> 00:00:06.715
the Lusail Stadium in Qatar.

00:00:06.715 --> 00:00:09.300
In just four years, it went from this...

00:00:09.884 --> 00:00:10.719
to this.

00:00:11.136 --> 00:00:13.346
It has 80,000 seats...

00:00:13.763 --> 00:00:16.975
and cost more than 
$700 million to build.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.228
It's the biggest stadium 
in the Middle East.

00:00:20.645 --> 00:00:23.064
But it's more than that.

00:00:23.064 --> 00:00:25.108
Over a decade ago, Qatar had hoped 

00:00:25.108 --> 00:00:28.611
that hosting a mega sporting event 
in stadiums like these 

00:00:28.611 --> 00:00:30.030
would put it on the map.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.491
But today, stadiums like Lusail
are a symbol

00:00:33.491 --> 00:00:35.952
of how a country can build 
grand infrastructure

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:38.038
to further its own interests

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:41.041
and how those ambitions can trap thousands.

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:45.128
"...migrant workers lost their lives
in the construction of the stadiums..."

00:00:45.128 --> 00:00:47.756
"...forced to labor under 
extremely poor conditions..."

00:00:47.756 --> 00:00:52.093
"Migrants working on infrastructure projects 
have died or suffered abuse."

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:59.642
"The logical source of oil for Europe 
is the Middle East..."

00:01:00.977 --> 00:01:05.690
Since the 1940s, when Qatar discovered 
huge reserves of oil and then gas

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.651
it's been one of the richest countries on Earth.

00:01:09.277 --> 00:01:13.782
70% of the government's revenue 
comes from oil and gas exports.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:15.658
Thanks to all that wealth

00:01:15.658 --> 00:01:18.495
Qatar has gone through a 
remarkable transformation.

00:01:19.204 --> 00:01:22.457
The capital, Doha, for instance, 
went from fishing town...

00:01:22.707 --> 00:01:26.086
to world class city in just 
a couple of decades

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:30.215
complete with extraordinary museums 
and fancy hotels.

00:01:30.882 --> 00:01:34.761
But in recent years, the world has been 
turning to renewable energy

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:37.555
making the oil and gas market
Qatar relies on

00:01:37.555 --> 00:01:38.890
even less dependable.

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:43.436
And in the Gulf, the country has seen 
regional tensions surge

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:46.773
most notably in 2017, when 
neighboring countries

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:48.817
issued a blockade against Qatar...

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:51.444
cutting off trade and travel.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:54.405
It cost the country hundreds 
of millions of dollars.

00:01:57.367 --> 00:01:59.828
So to diversify and protect 
its economy...

00:02:00.161 --> 00:02:02.705
Qatar has started leaning 
into another area

00:02:02.705 --> 00:02:05.250
that helped establish its status 
as a wealthy nation...

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:07.252
sports.

00:02:07.544 --> 00:02:09.754
That repositioning is 
based on soft power.

00:02:09.754 --> 00:02:12.549
Qatar is trying to secure friends 
when in need.

00:02:12.882 --> 00:02:14.509
Particularly powerful friends 
from the West

00:02:14.509 --> 00:02:16.136
like the United States and the UK.

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:17.303
And so the best way to do that

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:19.597
is through these soft sectors 
like sports.

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:24.769
In the last 18 years, Qatar has held 
more than 20 major sporting events.

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:29.315
But nothing compares to 
the 2022 World Cup.

00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:34.863
"The winner is... Qatar."

00:02:36.364 --> 00:02:39.284
Qatar won the rights to host 
the World Cup in 2010.

00:02:39.784 --> 00:02:42.745
"Today we celebrate, but tomorrow... 

00:02:43.037 --> 00:02:44.289
the work begins."

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:46.958
It had made an unbeatable 
promise to FIFA.

00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:48.793
It would spend more money

00:02:48.793 --> 00:02:50.837
than any host had ever done before.

00:02:51.296 --> 00:02:53.631
$200 billion.

00:02:53.631 --> 00:02:55.508
And it delivered.

00:02:56.134 --> 00:02:59.012
Since then, Qatar has 
built up 8 stadiums.

00:02:59.262 --> 00:03:02.932
It's built dozens of hotels and 
laid down miles of roads

00:03:02.932 --> 00:03:05.643
and metro lines to connect 
all that infrastructure.

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:08.646
They've even completed a whole new city

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:11.024
with golf courses, marinas,
and a theme park.

00:03:11.441 --> 00:03:13.860
It can brag that it is the first 
Middle Eastern country

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.612
that's ever hosted a mega sporting event.

00:03:15.612 --> 00:03:16.529
To be able to do that

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:19.449
you have a lot of partners, 
including FIFA itself

00:03:19.449 --> 00:03:23.578
and all the celebrity ambassadors 
who are vouching for Qatar

00:03:23.578 --> 00:03:27.749
having these incredible world class stadiums, 
these glowing, glistening hotels.

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:31.002
You can come to Doha and feel secure

00:03:31.002 --> 00:03:33.463
that you're experiencing a world 
that's business savvy

00:03:33.463 --> 00:03:34.756
that's modern and advanced.

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:37.592
While the spotlight was 
supposed to show Qatar

00:03:37.592 --> 00:03:39.636
as a shining example of development...

00:03:40.011 --> 00:03:43.056
it also exposed one of its darkest secrets.

00:03:46.935 --> 00:03:48.603
To power decades of development

00:03:48.603 --> 00:03:53.233
Qatar has relied on a constant supply 
of temporary, low paid workers

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:55.818
mainly from South Asia 
and East Africa.

00:03:56.527 --> 00:04:00.281
Since the early 2000s, so many foreigners 
have come to work in Qatar

00:04:00.281 --> 00:04:03.409
they now make up 80% 
of the total population.

00:04:03.826 --> 00:04:08.039
They build, clean, serve, and staff 
pretty much every industry.

00:04:08.539 --> 00:04:13.336
When we think about how migrant workers 
recruitment journey commences

00:04:13.336 --> 00:04:15.672
we think about workers who are

00:04:15.672 --> 00:04:18.591
in extremely low wage situations.

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:22.512
So they are looking for a place 
where they can emigrate...

00:04:23.012 --> 00:04:26.182
to allow them some upward 
socioeconomic mobility.

00:04:26.557 --> 00:04:29.102
And Qatar promises them that mobility.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:33.523
Typically, Qatari companies hire 
international recruitment agencies

00:04:33.523 --> 00:04:35.525
that find workers to send over.

00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:38.820
They promise these workers 
well-paying jobs

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.906
in exchange for illegal 
recruitment fees upfront.

00:04:42.407 --> 00:04:45.076
They also have the workers pay 
for medical tests

00:04:45.076 --> 00:04:47.453
passports, flights, the visa...

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:50.081
all to get the job contract.

00:04:50.081 --> 00:04:52.750
The workers often take out loans 
in their home countries

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:55.670
to pay the agencies incurring 
significant debt.

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:58.256
It's a huge transnational...

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:00.925
cross-border operation...

00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:04.929
which completely screws workers
at the start of their journey.

00:05:04.929 --> 00:05:08.808
Workers often use their family's 
savings to pay for a job

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:11.477
in one of the richest countries 
per capita in the world.

00:05:11.894 --> 00:05:13.855
They arrive in Qatar often to find

00:05:13.855 --> 00:05:16.065
they're not getting the job 
and salary promised

00:05:16.065 --> 00:05:17.942
and can't do anything about it...

00:05:18.401 --> 00:05:22.071
because they've now entered 
the country's kafala system.

00:05:22.739 --> 00:05:25.658
It's a uniquely restrictive 
immigration system

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:28.328
that can take different forms 
in different industries.

00:05:28.745 --> 00:05:31.289
But they all have one crucial thing 
in common.

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:35.001
Kafala legally ties the immigrant's 
immigration status

00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:36.461
to their employer.

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:38.171
Meaning an individual employer

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:39.630
has to sponsor their worker

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.342
which gives them an inordinate amount
of power over them.

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:45.470
They can control when 
their employees job ends

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:47.388
or if they can change jobs.

00:05:47.388 --> 00:05:50.975
They can control if and when 
migrants leave the country.

00:05:51.225 --> 00:05:53.311
Often by confiscating their passports.

00:05:53.811 --> 00:05:56.272
And they can also control the worker's ability

00:05:56.272 --> 00:05:58.316
to renew their residency and work permit.

00:05:59.067 --> 00:06:02.570
But beyond the restrictions on a migrant's 
freedom of movement

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:05.740
the system also traps them in a cycle of abuse.

00:06:06.074 --> 00:06:09.911
And that became evident when Qatar
started preparing for the World Cup.

00:06:12.914 --> 00:06:15.249
To deliver on the infrastructure 
Qatar promised 

00:06:15.249 --> 00:06:17.043
it tapped into its migrant pipeline

00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:19.962
and recruited hundreds of thousands 
of new workers.

00:06:20.296 --> 00:06:23.424
When Qatar was awarded
 the World Cup in 2010...

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:25.968
all of the people involved in decision making

00:06:25.968 --> 00:06:28.429
knew that the World Cup would be built

00:06:28.429 --> 00:06:30.556
based on the kafala system.

00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:33.684
Thousands of migrants turned into 
construction workers

00:06:33.684 --> 00:06:35.603
to build stadiums like Lusail

00:06:35.603 --> 00:06:38.147
which became the site 
for human rights abuses

00:06:38.147 --> 00:06:40.525
that ranged from 
verbal abuse to death.

00:06:41.442 --> 00:06:43.778
Several investigations into Qatar's stadiums

00:06:43.778 --> 00:06:46.364
revealed that employers
usually house workers

00:06:46.364 --> 00:06:48.783
in private camps far 
from city centers...

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:51.619
where they are forced to live 
in overcrowded rooms

00:06:51.619 --> 00:06:54.247
and unsanitary conditions.

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:57.041
And at work, their lives 
are put at risk.

00:06:57.583 --> 00:07:01.170
Migrant workers are often given 
the most dangerous jobs on the site

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:04.048
and can be forced to work up to 14 hour days

00:07:04.048 --> 00:07:05.675
in Qatar's extreme heat.

00:07:06.384 --> 00:07:08.928
The day this photo of the 
Lusail stadium was taken

00:07:08.928 --> 00:07:12.306
the high in Doha reached 
109 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:17.061
They have spent so many hours 
in the blistering sun...

00:07:17.478 --> 00:07:20.231
and after that they only permitted

00:07:20.231 --> 00:07:21.482
a five minute break to eat

00:07:21.482 --> 00:07:23.693
and they eat standing 
and then go back.

00:07:23.693 --> 00:07:26.737
Whenever you're also up on a scaffolding,
coming down for a bathroom break

00:07:26.737 --> 00:07:28.030
might get you in trouble with your boss.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:29.991
And so you end up drinking less water

00:07:29.991 --> 00:07:31.159
despite the need to have it

00:07:31.159 --> 00:07:32.910
to avoid heat exhaustion.

00:07:33.202 --> 00:07:35.204
The number of work-related
migrant deaths

00:07:35.204 --> 00:07:37.331
surrounding stadiums remains unclear

00:07:37.331 --> 00:07:39.375
due to Qatar's lack of transparency.

00:07:40.042 --> 00:07:43.171
In fact, that's the case for 
the entire kafala system.

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:45.673
But there have been some clues 
along the way.

00:07:46.424 --> 00:07:48.342
The death certificates Nepal
has received

00:07:48.342 --> 00:07:50.511
with the bodies sent back from Qatar

00:07:50.511 --> 00:07:53.431
show that nearly 120 Nepalese migrants 

00:07:53.431 --> 00:07:55.725
have died on the job since 2008.

00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:58.936
The real number, though, 
is likely much higher.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:03.483
In 2019, a study examined the link 
 between Nepalese worker deaths

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:05.902
and heat exposure and concluded heatstroke

00:08:05.902 --> 00:08:08.571
was a likely cause of cardiovascular deaths.

00:08:09.405 --> 00:08:13.075
That alone could raise the number 
of just Nepali migrant worker deaths

00:08:13.075 --> 00:08:14.494
to about 600.

00:08:15.495 --> 00:08:18.706
In addition to living and working 
in abusive conditions

00:08:18.706 --> 00:08:20.917
many are also not getting paid 
for their work.

00:08:21.626 --> 00:08:24.170
Workers have repeatedly faced wage theft.

00:08:24.378 --> 00:08:27.173
So then the debt keeps racking up

00:08:27.173 --> 00:08:31.052
because the debt still exists 
and in many cases

00:08:31.052 --> 00:08:35.097
workers have still not been able to 
survive on a daily basis.

00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:37.725
This is one of the richest countries 
in the world

00:08:37.725 --> 00:08:39.977
and yet they haven't been able to accelerate

00:08:39.977 --> 00:08:43.147
its reforms and ensuring people 
have been paid on time.

00:08:43.481 --> 00:08:46.108
Some workers try to file complaints, 
only to find

00:08:46.108 --> 00:08:48.236
there are few effective mechanisms in place.

00:08:48.861 --> 00:08:51.280
And speaking out comes with 
risk of retaliation...

00:08:51.822 --> 00:08:54.575
which has kept migrants isolated
in the kafala system

00:08:54.575 --> 00:08:58.120
with increasing debt and 
unable to access justice.

00:09:00.289 --> 00:09:01.916
After years of mounting pressure

00:09:01.916 --> 00:09:04.710
from human rights organizations 
and a formal complaint

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:07.547
that reached the UN's International 
Labor Organization...

00:09:07.964 --> 00:09:10.716
in 2017, Qatar signed 
an agreement

00:09:10.716 --> 00:09:13.344
promising to align its laws 
and labor practices

00:09:13.344 --> 00:09:15.263
to international standards.

00:09:15.263 --> 00:09:19.642
In 2020, 10 years after construction 
for the World Cup began

00:09:19.642 --> 00:09:22.603
Qatar implemented a minimum wage 
for workers

00:09:22.603 --> 00:09:24.313
and now allows them to terminate

00:09:24.313 --> 00:09:25.940
their employment contract.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:26.524
But...

00:09:26.524 --> 00:09:29.110
The challenge with the minimum wage 
laws is enforcement.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:31.362
Employers started pushing back 
against the government.

00:09:31.362 --> 00:09:35.992
And so even though we haven't seen 
amendments to the legislation.

00:09:35.992 --> 00:09:39.370
Indeed, we're seeing a watering down 
of reforms.

00:09:39.829 --> 00:09:43.040
And for the migrants who have already 
been abused to the kafala system.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.251
This is all too late.

00:09:45.251 --> 00:09:47.503
Civil rights groups and 
football associations

00:09:47.503 --> 00:09:49.255
are now calling for a remedy fund

00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:51.674
to compensate the migrant workers financially.

00:09:52.091 --> 00:09:54.468
And some teams have 
taken a stand in the field...

00:09:54.927 --> 00:09:56.470
and on social media.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:57.680
"We have learned that the decision

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:59.348
to host the World Cup in Qatar

00:09:59.348 --> 00:10:00.600
which resulted in the suffering

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.852
and harm of countless of 
our fellow workers..."

00:10:03.603 --> 00:10:05.271
Making this year's World Cup

00:10:05.271 --> 00:10:07.273
a particularly difficult one to watch.

00:10:08.983 --> 00:10:10.359
Millions of football fans

00:10:10.359 --> 00:10:13.696
will still tune in and cheer 
for their favorite teams.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:16.907
But after that final match is over...

00:10:16.907 --> 00:10:18.618
and the trophy is awarded...

00:10:18.701 --> 00:10:22.997
stadiums like Lusail will continue 
to be a grim reminder

00:10:22.997 --> 00:10:25.249
that it all came at 
a human cost.

